          "description": "Detect duplicated RAW DATA inside a supplementary data file (.xlsx/.xlsm/.csv/.tsv/.docx). Give it the FILE PATH — it loads the grid itself; never retype cells. Run it on every participant-level supplement (the ones extract_supplement flags as having many rows). It finds: duplicated participant rows (>=2 identical high-precision values in the same columns, within AND across sheets — recycled rows between \"independent\" sheets are the strongest signal), repeated vertical runs (fill-down/block copy-paste), and improbably specific values recurring. Matches are weighted by significant-digit information content (a match on 123.456 is evidence; a match on 100 is nothing), with automatic discounts for derived values (fractions, sqrt, log — their precision is an artifact), sorted/ID runs, and values many rows share; repeat counts are gated on a chance model, so expected coincidences are not reported. Severity caps at highly_suspicious: duplication is improbable, never impossible. Pass exclude_columns for columns that are shared BY DESIGN — IDs, codes, coordinates, site/plot-level covariates (weather, species constants), group labels. When in doubt, leave a column IN and judge the caveats. Set data_context=\"pooled_or_meta_analysis\" when the file compiles data from OTHER papers — pooled rows duplicate by construction and the tool then refuses to accuse (indeterminate cap). Parameters: path (str), sheet (str, optional: one worksheet), exclude_columns (list of column names), data_context (str: raw_participant_level | pooled_or_meta_analysis | unknown)",
